\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The single-link factor and its maximizers}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For the reduced weight in \ref{fdip-010Y} over #{k}, the factor
#{B_e(v)=b_e(\phi^\circ,v\phi^\circ)} depends only on the double
coset #{G^\circ vG^\circ}. In particular #{B_e} is constant on
#{G^\circ}. In a positive definite real inner product space with
isometric action and hopping factor
#{b_e(o,o')=\ue^{w_e\langle o,o'\rangle}}, #{w_e>0}, the set of
group elements maximizing #{B_e} is exactly #{G^\circ}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{a,b\in G^\circ}, the source factor #{b} fixes
#{\phi^\circ}, and diagonal invariance of the edge factor removes
the head factor #{a} from #{avb\phi^\circ} since #{a^{-1}}
also fixes the reference scalar. Thus #{B_e(avb)=B_e(v)}.
The double coset of the identity is #{G^\circ} itself. In the
real hopping case,
##{\frac{B_e(1)}{B_e(v)}
  =\ue^{\frac{w_e}{2}\|v\phi^\circ-\phi^\circ\|^2}.}
The two orbit points have equal norm. Positivity of #{w_e} and
of the inner product makes the ratio at least one with equality
precisely when #{v\phi^\circ=\phi^\circ}. A general edge factor
can have additional maximizers even over the reals, as the
constant factor #{1} shows.}
}

\p{The factor #{B_e} is the link mass term in the reduced weight.
A pure-gauge function of one link with distinct endpoints, invariant
under all vertex gauges, must be constant. A gauge at the head
alone can carry any link value to any other. On a loop the action
is conjugation, so every class function is allowed. For instance
the indicator of the identity is nonconstant for any nontrivial
group over a nonzero ring.}
